        <h3>Overhand (Pronated) Grip</h3>
        <p>Palms face away from you. Overhand is the standard dead hang grip and matches the pull-up.</p>
        <p>Use this grip for general <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength training</a> and pull-up preparation.</p>

        <h3>Underhand (Supinated) Grip</h3>
        <p>Palms face toward you. Many people find underhand hangs more comfortable on the wrists. An underhand grip puts the biceps tendon under stretch. Stop if the front of the elbow hurts. Use this grip for variety when your forearms need a change.</p>

        <h3>Neutral Grip</h3>
        <p>Palms face each other on parallel handles. Neutral grip suits people who find overhand uncomfortable. Ask a clinician first if you have wrist pain or are recovering from a shoulder problem.</p>

        <h3>Mixed Grip</h3>
        <p>One hand overhand and one hand underhand. Mixed grip is an optional variation. Keep the underhand elbow straight and stop if the front of the elbow hurts. Alternate which hand goes over and under between sets.</p>
        <p>Read more about <a href="/mixed-grip-hang/">mixed grip dead hangs</a>.</p>

        <h2>Breathing During Dead Hangs</h2>
        <p>Breathe slowly and steadily throughout the hang. Slow breathing helps many people stay relaxed during a hang.</p>
        <p>Inhale and exhale at a pace that feels natural and without pausing between breaths.</p>
        <p>Do not hold your breath during a dead hang. Ask your doctor first if you have high blood pressure or a heart condition.</p>

        <h2>Sets, Reps and Duration</h2>
        <p>Perform 2 to 4 sets per session. Hold each set for about 10 to 60 seconds based on your current capacity. Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out. Rest 60 to 90 seconds between sets. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days and add a session only if you stay pain free. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>

