            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Beginner</td><td>10 to 20 seconds</td><td>3</td><td>90 seconds</td><td>3 days a week</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Intermediate</td><td>20 to 45 seconds</td><td>3 to 4</td><td>60 to 90 seconds</td><td>3 to 4 days a week if pain free</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Advanced</td><td>45 to 90 seconds</td><td>3 to 4</td><td>60 seconds</td><td>4 days a week or more if pain free</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </div>

        <p>Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly. Do this roughly once a week. Add external load only after a comfortable and pain-free bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds. View the structured <a href="/training-programs/">training programs</a> for detailed weekly plans.</p>

        <h2>Common Form Mistakes</h2>
        <p>The most common errors are bent elbows, shrugged shoulders, swinging and holding your breath. Each shortens your hold and can make your shoulders and elbows sore.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Bent elbows:</strong> Straighten your arms fully without forcing them past straight.</li>
          <li><strong>Shrugged shoulders:</strong> Relax the shoulders for a passive hang. Pull them down and back for an active hang.</li>
          <li><strong>Swinging:</strong> Engage your core lightly, keep your legs together and start each hang from a dead-still position.</li>
          <li><strong>Breath holding:</strong> Breathe slowly and do not hold your breath.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Read more about <a href="/deadhang-mistakes/">dead hang form mistakes</a> and how to fix them.</p>

        <h2>Modifications for Beginners</h2>

        <h3>Feet-on-Ground Hang</h3>
        <p>Place a box beneath the bar. Keep your toes in contact with the surface. Gradually shift more weight onto your hands. Foot support takes part of your weight.</p>

        <h3>Band-Assisted Hang</h3>
        <p>Loop a resistance band over the bar and step into it. A loop band takes part of your weight. Thicker bands take more. Use progressively thinner bands as your strength increases. Record the assist you used so tests stay comparable.</p>

        <h3>Flexed-Arm Hang</h3>
        <p>Hang with elbows bent at 90 degrees. This regression builds biceps and forearm strength before you attempt a full dead hang. Hold for 10 to 20 seconds. Move to straight arms once you can hold 20 seconds consistently. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>

        <p>Full regression protocols are covered in the <a href="/beginner-deadhang/">beginner dead hang program</a>.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Should I use an overhand or underhand grip for dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Overhand grip is the standard choice for dead hangs and matches the pull-up. An underhand grip puts the biceps tendon under stretch. Stop if the front of the elbow hurts. Some people alternate between grips across sessions for variety.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How long should a beginner dead hang for?</h3>
          <p>A common starting point is 10 to 20 seconds per hang for 3 sets with 90 seconds of rest between sets. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly. Do this roughly once a week. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Can I dead hang every day?</h3>
          <p>Some experienced people hang lightly most days. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days. Add a session only if you stay pain free. Leave at least one rest day between hard sessions. Take extra rest if you feel persistent forearm soreness or joint discomfort.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What if I cannot hang for more than 5 seconds?</h3>
          <p>Start with your feet on the ground using a box beneath the bar. Keep your toes in contact and shift weight onto your hands gradually. A resistance band looped over the bar also takes part of your weight. Both methods let you practice the hanging position while you build grip strength.</p>
        </div>

